{
  "_comment": [
    "OUR settings -- the ones we maintain, as opposed to what the VESC detection wizard measures.",
    "Written 2026-10-20. Rule by family: every l_*, s_pid_* and si_* field is OURS, plus the four",
    "foc_* mode fields listed under shared_mc. Everything else beginning foc_ is the wizard's.",
    "severity 'functional' changes how the wheel drives; 'cosmetic' only affects VESC Tool's own",
    "display and reaches nothing we consume.",
    "DO NOT write this file back wholesale to an ESC -- check_ours.py reports, set_mcconf_field.py",
    "writes named fields only.",
    "shared_mc_other / shared_app_other were generated 2026-10-20 from FL after its lambda run, and every value in them is identical on all four Sep 26 configs. Together with shared_mc, cosmetic_mc, shared_app, per_wheel and wizard_mc they cover EVERY field on the board.",
    "2026-10-24: per_wheel 10/12/13 moved 250W -> 350W with limits 14/-14/20/15, matching FL. ALL FOUR MOTORS ARE NOW 350W -- measured, not assumed: flux_linkage reads 0.016191 (FR) / 0.015439 (FL) / 0.015730 (RR) / 0.016693 (RL), all clustered on FL's 350W signature 0.015632 and all ~40% above the 250W band 0.0104-0.0116. The boards already carried 14/-14/20/15; it was this file that was stale, so the four 'functional deviations' it reported on FR/RR/RL were false. Also 2026-10-24: m_invert_direction written 1 -> 0 on FR and RR (it was 1 on all four, so the right pair ran backwards against the left) -- confirmed twice, by the operator watching each wheel and by the mirroring rule here. Corner <-> node mapping VERIFIED against turning wheels for the first time this day: 10=FR 11=FL 12=RR 13=RL, each spun alone over its own USB port."
  ],
